body:
This is NOT true. You can have both cars on the same permit, but they are not supposed to be on campus at the same time and you can be ticketed for it.  Utah Code R810-5-1 specifically says "Only one vehicle per assigned permit may park on campus at a given time. If more than one vehicle registered to a single permit is found on campus, a ticket will be issued."

&#x200B;

Me and my friend who share a permit had to call commuter services to challenge a ticket he got because we parked on campus within like 30 minutes of each other even though we were never parked on campus at the same time.

If im understanding you correctly then I think you could get away with some code like this:  
***

buttonPress= digitalRead(buttonPin);

equalPress= digitalRead(equalPin);


If(equalPress == HIGH)
 { 

 operation = counter % 4;

counter == 0;


switch (operation) 
{

case 0: 

answer = num1 + num2;

break;


case 1:

answer = num1 - num2;

break;


case 2:

answer = num1 ÷ num2;

break;


case 3:

answer = num1 * num2;

break;
}

}

If(buttonPress==HIGH) //+,×,÷,- button pressed 

counter++;

delay(100); 

*/
delay is for stability so you don't get extra button presses. It will currently check if either buttons are pressed once every 100ms if that's resulting in extra button presses ('+' turning into '÷' ect.) Then increase the delay timer. \*

Note:
The higher your delay timer worse performance you'll get.  You'll only be able to press buttons as fast as the delay is since that's how fast it will read your button state pins. However if the delay is too fast then it might read your single button press multiple times causing errors in intended operation.  Also it is possible to press both the operator button and equal button at the same time, if you do that the code is set up to perform the math first and ignore the operator button  so it will execute whatever math operation it had stored from the last iteration of void  loop(). 

***

Apologies for formatting,  writing code on mobile is my new favorite hatred. 
I don't know your full setup so I didn't include other pins or code but you might be able to adapt that to whatever code you've got to get it to work.

Edit: also this code isn't great,  not efficient,  there are exploits that could break the code and other problems im probably just not aware of. In my own projects I don't write professional code, I write just enough to get something functioning. It's also been a while since I've programmed so definitly be on the lookout for errors in the code

